\citation{powell1998direct}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Approximation ratio comparison between classical baseline algorithms and single-layer QAOA for combinatorial optimization. The approximation ratio measures the quality of solutions relative to the optimal value, with higher ratios indicating better performance. Classical baselines include \textbf  {uniform random} sampling (0.50), \textbf  {biased random} sampling toward better solutions (0.618), and \textbf  {linear programming (LP) rounding} (0.632). The \textbf  {hybrid approach} combines classical preprocessing with quantum optimization, achieving 0.75. \textbf  {QAOA with p=1} matches the hybrid performance at 0.75, demonstrating that single-layer quantum circuits can compete with sophisticated classical-quantum hybrid methods. This result establishes a baseline for quantum advantage claims, showing that even shallow quantum circuits achieve approximation ratios comparable to advanced classical heuristics. The gap between simple classical methods (0.50-0.63) and quantum-enhanced approaches (0.75) illustrates the potential benefit of quantum optimization for NP-hard combinatorial problems.}}{4}{}}
\newlabel{fig:approx-ratios}{{1}{4}{}{}{}}
